ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: yellow
MANDATORY_BASELINES: [{"name": "Downstream Validation (sklearn.utils.check_array)", "why": "Standard ML pipelines validate inputs; NaNs/Infs are usually rejected immediately, making the failure loud, not silent.", "repo_or_cite": "scikit-learn"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Classifying Inf/NaN generation as a 'silent' failure. While the .mean() call does not raise a Python exception, Inf/NaN are standard IEEE-754 poison pills that cause immediate, loud crashes in almost all downstream ML models (e.g., ValueError in sklearn).", "Equating numerical overflow (a known property of fp16) with a semantic cross-tool divergence."]   MISSING_METRICS: ["Downstream pipeline survival rate (does the pipeline actually complete and train a silently degraded model, or does it crash 100% of the time?)"]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Feed the pandas Series.mean() result (Inf/NaN) into a standard downstream ML pipeline step (e.g., sklearn StandardScaler or any estimator). If the pipeline raises a loud exception (ValueError: Input contains NaN, infinity or a value too large), then the threat model of a 'silent cross-tool divergence' collapses into a localized, easily caught overflow bug.
RATIONALE: The empirical divergence (pandas avoiding upcast vs. numpy upcasting) is a verified and fascinating quirk of the nanops implementation. However, the claim relies heavily on the premise that this divergence is "silent." In data engineering, returning NaN/Inf is rarely silent; it aggressively breaks downstream components. Unless the killer experiment shows this NaN/Inf bypasses standard validation and silently corrupts model weights, this is just a documented artifact of fp16 overflow behavior rather than a novel pipeline vulnerability.
